Subject: [PATCH 1/3] pci/probe: adopt orphaned PCIe Express capability

Some devices (notably AMD RDNA4 / Navi 48 = R9700) populate the PCIe
Express capability structure (cap ID 0x10) in standard config space at a
fixed offset, but fail to link it into the legacy capability chain
reachable via PCI_CAPABILITY_LIST.

On the AMD R9700 specifically, the PCIe cap lives at offset 0x64 but
the cap chain at 0x40 walks 0x40 -> 0x48 (vendor) -> 0x50 (PM, next=0xa0)
-> 0xa0 (MSI) -> 0x00, skipping 0x64 entirely. This is a spec violation
on AMD's part; Linux is spec-conformant in not finding it.

Without this fallback, pci_find_capability(PCI_CAP_ID_EXP) returns 0,
set_pcie_port_type() bails before setting dev->pcie_cap, and the device
is mis-classified as conventional PCI. That means:
  - PCIe extended config space (offset >= 0x100) is never probed
  - AER, L1 PM Substates, and Resizable BAR caps are never enumerated
  - amdgpu cannot resize BAR0, etc.

Windows and macOS both probe more permissively and find the cap. This
patch scans 0x40..0xFC as a last-resort fallback when the legacy chain
walk fails. See companion patch to drivers/pci/pci.c that lets
pci_find_capability() return dev->pcie_cap on subsequent calls.

Apply to: drivers/pci/probe.c

--- a/drivers/pci/probe.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/probe.c
@@ -1620,6 +1620,33 @@
 }

+/*
+ * Some devices (notably AMD RDNA4 / Navi48) populate the PCIe capability
+ * structure in standard config space but fail to link it into the legacy
+ * capability chain reachable via PCI_CAPABILITY_LIST. Without a chained
+ * link, pci_find_capability() returns 0 and the device is mis-classified
+ * as conventional PCI -- which means PCIe extended config space (>=0x100)
+ * is never probed, and capabilities such as AER, L1 PM Substates and
+ * Resizable BAR are never enumerated. Scan for an orphaned cap as a
+ * last-resort fallback. This matches the more permissive cap-discovery
+ * used by other PCIe-aware OSes (notably Windows).
+ */
+static int pci_scan_orphan_pcie_cap(struct pci_dev *pdev)
+{
+	int pos;
+	u32 header;
+
+	for (pos = PCI_STD_HEADER_SIZEOF; pos <= PCI_CFG_SPACE_SIZE - 4; pos += 4) {
+		if (pci_read_config_dword(pdev, pos, &header))
+			continue;
+		if ((header & 0xff) == PCI_CAP_ID_EXP)
+			return pos;
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
+
+
 void set_pcie_port_type(struct pci_dev *pdev)
 {
 	int pos;
@@ -1648,7 +1675,15 @@ void set_pcie_port_type(struct pci_dev *pdev)
 	struct pci_dev *parent;

 	pos = pci_find_capability(pdev, PCI_CAP_ID_EXP);
-	if (!pos)
-		return;
+	if (!pos) {
+		pos = pci_scan_orphan_pcie_cap(pdev);
+		if (!pos)
+			return;
+		pci_info(pdev,
+			 "found orphaned PCIe capability at 0x%02x (not in legacy cap chain) - adopting as PCIe\n",
+			 pos);
+	}

 	pdev->pcie_cap = pos;
 	pci_read_config_word(pdev, pos + PCI_EXP_FLAGS, &reg16);

NOTE: Line numbers in this patch reflect the 7.0.0-pcifix kernel tree.
For a different upstream version, apply the changes by searching for
the function `set_pcie_port_type` and:

  1. Insert the new `pci_scan_orphan_pcie_cap()` function (and its
     preceding comment block) right before `set_pcie_port_type`.
  2. Inside `set_pcie_port_type`, find the existing block:
         pos = pci_find_capability(pdev, PCI_CAP_ID_EXP);
         if (!pos)
             return;
     and replace `if (!pos) return;` with the fallback block from this
     patch (call pci_scan_orphan_pcie_cap, pci_info on success).
